From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase, kernel32: application restart and recovery

RegisterApplicationRestart and RegisterApplicationRecoveryCallback kept
nothing, GetApplicationRestartSettings was E_NOTIMPL,
GetApplicationRecoveryCallback and UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback
were missing, and ApplicationRecoveryInProgress/Finished failed. Office,
browsers and editors register both so that a crash loses no work.

- The registrations are kept in a block PEB->WerRegistrationData points
  to, laid out alike for 32- and 64-bit processes, so another process's can
  be read (GetApplicationRestartSettings and GetApplicationRecoveryCallback
  on another process; a 64-bit process from a 32-bit one is out of reach).
  Windows' limits: command lines under RESTART_MAX_CMD_LINE, known restart
  flags, ping intervals up to five minutes (0 for the default 5 s);
  ERROR_NOT_FOUND when nothing is registered, ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER with
  the length when the buffer is short.
- When the process crashes (an unhandled exception, past the top-level
  filter), the recovery callback runs on a thread of its own for as long as
  it pings within its interval or until ApplicationRecoveryFinished; then a
  program registered for restart, which had run at least a minute and did
  not ask for RESTART_NO_CRASH, is started again with its command line, as
  the error reporting does.
- The functions move from kernel32 to kernelbase (kernel32 imports them).

Gate: test/apprestart-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit; ~70 s for the restart
after a minute). Mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_APP_RESTART, SG_MUTANT_RESTART_AT_ONCE,
SG_MUTANT_NO_CRASH_RECOVERY (kernelbase/debug.c).
